{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "gr-wa",
  "connector": "は",
  "gloss": "as for… (the topic)",
  "title": "は — the topic: as for…",
  "pattern": "topic は comment",
  "explanation": "は (said “wa”) marks what you’re talking about, and the rest of the sentence says something about it: {今日|きょう}は{休|やす}みです — today, I’m off. Think “as for…”. It’s written は but always said wa as a particle. Once the topic is clear, Japanese leaves it out: there’s no need to keep saying {私|わたし}は.",
  "natural": "Good for introducing yourself, for contrasts (コーヒーは{好|す}きです — coffee, I do like), and for short questions: お{名前|なまえ}は？ — and your name?",
  "stiff": "Starting every sentence with {私|わたし}は sounds stiff and foreign. Say it once; after that, leave it out.",
  "examples": [
    {
      "id": "gx-wa-1",
      "japanese": "今日は休みです。",
      "furigana": "{今日|きょう}は{休|やす}みです。",
